body:
@jpzwarte I looked into this. `referenceTarget` does not introduce new accessibility-testing limitations beyond those that already exist with closed shadow roots.

When a shadow root is `open`, page-context accessibility tools (e.g. axe-core) can read author-provided semantics, including those of referenced targets. When a shadow root is `closed`, those tools cannot access ARIA properties inside the shadow tree -- but this is already true today for *all* elements in closed shadow roots, independent of `referenceTarget`.

The key distinction from `ElementInternals` is scope and agency. Closed shadow roots affect a relatively small fraction of cases (~6% per Deque's estimate), and authors can choose to make a shadow root `open`. In practice, frameworks that prioritize accessibility tend to do so. By contrast, **all custom elements using `ElementInternals` currently trigger false positives**, and authors have no opt-out: adopting `ElementInternals` necessarily comes with accessibility testing errors, even when authors intend to do the right thing.

@lukewarlow  said
> Does it make sense to have an opt-out? If authors don't want this exposed sometimes then we'd realistically have to make this an opt-in because we shouldn't suddenly expose it.

I've been given this more thoughts and discussed it with other folks offline and we all arrived independently at the same conclusion as you. The opt-out should be unnecessary, and it'd be better if all ElementInternals always exposed their readonly internals accessibility semantics anyway. If this opt-out is removed, then `usesElementInternals` becomes unnecessary as well, and the proposal above becomes much simpler: a read-only getter for accessibility attributes set on the internals object.
